theory.intro:
A point on the Cartesian plane is written as an **ordered pair** $(x, y)$: the $x$-coordinate first (across), then the $y$-coordinate (up or down). Coordinates need not be whole numbers — a point can sit between the gridlines.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[step=0.5,gray!18] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[step=1,gray!40] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->,thick] (-3.4,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,thick] (0,-3.4)--(0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,gray!70] (1.5,0)--(1.5,2);
\draw[dashed,gray!70] (0,2)--(1.5,2);
\fill (1.5,2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[above right,font=\footnotesize] at (1.5,2) {$P(a,\,b)$};
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (1.5,-0.08) {$a$};
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-0.08,2) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
Points with the **same $x$-coordinate** line up vertically; points with the **same $y$-coordinate** line up horizontally.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[step=1,gray!40] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[<->,thick] (-3.4,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,thick] (0,-3.4)--(0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,gray!70] (-2,-1.6)--(-2,2.6);
\fill (-2,2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2.15,2) {$A$};
\fill (-2,-1) circle (2.2pt);
\node[left,font=\footnotesize] at (-2.15,-1) {$B$};
\draw[dashed,gray!70] (0.6,-2)--(2.9,-2);
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (1,-2.15) {$C$};
\fill (2.5,-2) circle (2.2pt);
\node[below,font=\footnotesize] at (2.5,-2.15) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
